Scott's Proposition 6.4 states that if \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) are information systems then so is \(\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\), and there are approximable \(\mathrm{inl}:\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathrm{inr}:\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\) such that for approximable \(f:\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{C}\) and \(g:\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C}\) there is a unique approximable \([f,g]:\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C}\) with \([f,g]\circ\mathrm{inl}=f\), \([f,g]\circ\mathrm{inr}=g\), and \([f,g](\bot)=\bot\). Clause (1) is \(v\ \mathrm{inl}\ u\) if and only if \(\{(X,\Delta)\mid X\in v\}\) entails \(u\); clause (2) is the right-hand copy \(\{(\Delta,Y)\mid Y\in w\}\); clause (3) is \(u\ [f,g]\ s\) if and only if \(\mathbf{C}\) entails \(s\) from \(\emptyset\), or the left projection of \(u\) is nonempty and is \(f\)-related to \(s\), or the right projection is nonempty and is \(g\)-related to \(s\). `inlMap` relates consistent \(v\) to sum-consistent \(u\) exactly when the right projection of \(u\) is empty and \(\mathbf{A}\) entails that left projection from \(v\), and `inrMap` is the symmetric clause with the sides exchanged. A left-only premise entails a sum set precisely when the right projection is empty and the left projection is entailed, because a left token is entailed only from a nonempty left projection and a right token is not entailed from a left-only set, so the two injections match (1) and (2), the extra consistency conjuncts being those an approximable relation must carry. `copairMap` is clause (3), including the empty-entailment alternative. `comp_copairMap_inlMap` and `comp_copairMap_inrMap` are the two composition equations, `copairMap_botElement` is the bottom equation, and `copairMap_unique` is uniqueness from those three hypotheses. `Basic` restates that universal property; the named `theorem_64` in `Proposition64` states only the uniqueness clause. Scott leaves the proof that definition 6.3 satisfies the axioms of 2.1 to the reader, and none of these glosses states or proves that \(\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\) is an information system. The check that (1)–(3) are approximable is written out. For `inlMap`, the empty relation is empty entailment, closure under union on the right uses Proposition 2.3(vi) on the entailments and 2.3(ii) with the subset axiom for consistency of the united left projections, and monotonicity uses `rht_eq_empty_of_sumEntSet`, two applications of 2.3(iv), and `lft_mem_Con_of_SumCon_rht_empty`; `inrMap` uses `lft_eq_empty_of_sumEntSet`, `entSet_rht_of_sumEntSet`, and `rht_mem_Con_of_SumCon_lft_empty`. Right-union for `copairMap` is `copair_union_disj`: two empty entailments combine by 2.3(vi), an empty entailment against a left or right relation is reduced by monotonicity to a relation on the empty input and then united, two left relations unite in \(f\) and two right relations unite in \(g\), and a left relation against a right relation is impossible because sum consistency forces one of those projections to be empty. Each composite proof is extensionality of the relation. Forward, the empty-entailment branch of (3) returns the original relation by monotonicity from the empty relation, the matching side returns it by monotonicity along the recorded entailment using 2.3(iii), and the opposite side contradicts emptiness of that projection in the injection witness. Backward, the bottom token of the domain is inserted so the relevant projection becomes nonempty, the enlarged set still entails the original set by reflexivity, and monotonicity supplies the relation that the nonempty branch of (3) requires; Scott does not mention the insertion, and it establishes the same equation when the input is empty. Uniqueness in `copairMap_unique` splits on `lftFinset_eq_empty_or_mem` and `rhtFinset_eq_empty_or_mem`. Both projections empty: `subset_botElement_of_projections_empty` puts the set under bottom, and `entSet_empty_of_rel_bot_like` with the bottom hypothesis yields empty entailment in \(\mathbf{C}\). Right projection nonempty and left empty: `entSet_liftRht_of_right_only` and monotonicity move the relation onto the right lift, the \(\mathrm{inr}\) equation gives \(g\), and the right branch of (3) applies. Left projection nonempty: sum consistency forces the right projection empty, and the same argument with `entSet_liftLft_of_left_only` gives \(f\). The converse direction unpacks a composite witness by `entSet_u_of_inl_witness` or `entSet_u_of_inr_witness` and transfers it by monotonicity. Scott's stated reason, that apart from bottom every element lies in the image of \(\mathrm{inl}\) or of \(\mathrm{inr}\), is the same trichotomy at element level: `eq_botElement_of_no_injections`, `not_mem_right_of_mem_left`, `inlMap_toElement_sumElementLft` (the left copy `sumElementLft` is sent by \(\mathrm{inl}\) back to the element), and `inrMap_toElement_sumElementRht`. **Theorem:** partial — the injections, copairing, the three equations, and uniqueness match, but the claim that \(\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\) is an information system is absent, and `theorem_64` records only uniqueness. **Proof:** yes — approximability of (1)–(3) and the three equations are checked by the half-wise entailment argument Scott sketches, and both the element trichotomy he cites and the corresponding split on projections are proved.
